1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,596
<font color="#FFFFFF">This work,</font>

2
00:00:04,676 --> 00:00:08,771
<font color="#FFFFFF">presented at NeurIPS 2022 by Johan Larsson and
Jonas Wallin of Lund University,</font>

3
00:00:08,851 --> 00:00:12,947
<font color="#FFFFFF">introduces the Hessian Screening Rule,</font>

4
00:00:13,027 --> 00:00:17,122
<font color="#FFFFFF">a new way to speed up fitting the lasso
regularization path.</font>

5
00:00:17,202 --> 00:00:21,298
<font color="#FFFFFF">Screening rules discard predictors before a model
is fit, shrinking the problem.</font>

6
00:00:21,378 --> 00:00:25,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors show that using second-order Hessian
information yields both far tighter screening</font>

7
00:00:25,553 --> 00:00:29,649
<font color="#FFFFFF">and much more accurate warm starts,</font>

8
00:00:29,729 --> 00:00:33,824
<font color="#FFFFFF">especially when predictors are highly correlated,
where existing rules struggle most.</font>

9
00:00:33,904 --> 00:00:38,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is a method that is the fastest across
nearly every simulated and real benchmark they tested.</font>

10
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:42,943
<font color="#FFFFFF">Sparse regression with the lasso is a workhorse for
high-dimensional data,</font>

11
00:00:43,023 --> 00:00:44,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">but fitting it is expensive.</font>

12
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:47,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">The best penalty strength is never known in advance,</font>

13
00:00:48,042 --> 00:00:54,361
<font color="#FFFFFF">so practitioners fit an entire path of models
across many penalty values and tune by cross-validation,</font>

14
00:00:54,441 --> 00:00:55,993
<font color="#FFFFFF">refitting again and again.</font>

15
00:00:56,073 --> 00:01:00,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">Screening rules help by discarding predictors
before the solver even runs,</font>

16
00:01:00,716 --> 00:01:02,267
<font color="#FFFFFF">shrinking each subproblem.</font>

17
00:01:02,347 --> 00:01:07,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trouble is that the widely used rules become
conservative and inefficient precisely</font>

18
00:01:07,805 --> 00:01:10,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">when predictors are strongly correlated,</font>

19
00:01:10,315 --> 00:01:13,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is exactly the regime where speed matters most.</font>

20
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:19,676
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors start from a unifying observation: the
popular strong rule and the working-set</font>

21
00:01:19,756 --> 00:01:23,553
<font color="#FFFFFF">strategy can both be expressed as estimates of the
gradient,</font>

22
00:01:23,633 --> 00:01:26,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">or correlation, at the next step of the path.</font>

23
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:31,242
<font color="#FFFFFF">When they lean only on first-order information,
these estimates are crude,</font>

24
00:01:31,322 --> 00:01:33,439
<font color="#FFFFFF">especially under high correlation.</font>

25
00:01:33,519 --> 00:01:35,313
<font color="#FFFFFF">That crudeness has two costs.</font>

26
00:01:35,393 --> 00:01:40,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">Screening becomes conservative, keeping far more
predictors than necessary,</font>

27
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:44,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the warm starts that seed each optimization are
inaccurate,</font>

28
00:01:44,310 --> 00:01:47,397
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the solver needs many more passes to converge.</font>

29
00:01:47,477 --> 00:01:52,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both problems point to the same fix, richer
curvature information from the Hessian.</font>

30
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:56,474
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their contribution is the Hessian Screening Rule.</font>

31
00:01:56,554 --> 00:01:59,888
<font color="#FFFFFF">It exploits second-order information in two
complementary ways.</font>

32
00:01:59,968 --> 00:02:03,302
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

33
00:02:03,382 --> 00:02:06,717
<font color="#FFFFFF">the Hessian gives a sharper estimate of the
correlation at the next penalty value,</font>

34
00:02:06,797 --> 00:02:10,131
<font color="#FFFFFF">which translates into far tighter screening.</font>

35
00:02:10,211 --> 00:02:13,545
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

36
00:02:13,625 --> 00:02:16,959
<font color="#FFFFFF">the same Hessian and its inverse yield a warm start
that is nearly the exact solution</font>

37
00:02:17,039 --> 00:02:20,373
<font color="#FFFFFF">whenever the active set does not change,</font>

38
00:02:20,453 --> 00:02:23,787
<font color="#FFFFFF">cutting the number of solver passes dramatically.</font>

39
00:02:23,867 --> 00:02:27,202
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors also show how to update the Hessian and
its inverse efficiently as the active set changes,</font>

40
00:02:27,282 --> 00:02:30,616
<font color="#FFFFFF">extend the method to general smooth convex losses
like logistic regression,</font>

41
00:02:30,696 --> 00:02:34,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">and release a full C++ and R implementation.</font>

42
00:02:34,410 --> 00:02:38,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method rests on a simple fact: on any interval
where the active set of nonzero coefficients is unchanged,</font>

43
00:02:38,470 --> 00:02:42,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">the lasso solution is a linear function of the penalty λ.</font>

44
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:46,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">That linearity lets the authors write down a
second-order estimate of the correlation</font>

45
00:02:46,590 --> 00:02:50,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">at the next penalty value using the Hessian of the
active predictors.</font>

46
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:54,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">To keep it cheap,</font>

47
00:02:54,710 --> 00:02:58,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">they restrict the expensive inner products to the
strong-rule set and add a small fraction</font>

48
00:02:58,770 --> 00:03:02,750
<font color="#FFFFFF">of the unit bound as a safety margin.</font>

49
00:03:02,830 --> 00:03:06,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">The very same Hessian inverse provides the warm start,</font>

50
00:03:06,890 --> 00:03:10,870
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is exact when the active set does not change,</font>

51
00:03:10,950 --> 00:03:14,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the solver often converges in a single pass.</font>

52
00:03:15,010 --> 00:03:18,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">Efficient low-rank updates keep the Hessian and its
inverse current as predictors enter and leave,</font>

53
00:03:19,070 --> 00:03:23,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">and an approximate-homotopy scheme adaptively
chooses the penalty grid.</font>

54
00:03:23,430 --> 00:03:26,674
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments cover both simulated and real data.</font>

55
00:03:26,754 --> 00:03:28,629
<font color="#FFFFFF">On simulated Gaussian designs,</font>

56
00:03:28,709 --> 00:03:34,559
<font color="#FFFFFF">they sweep a low-dimensional regime with ten
thousand observations and a hundred predictors</font>

57
00:03:34,639 --> 00:03:40,489
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a high-dimensional regime with four hundred
observations and forty thousand predictors,</font>

58
00:03:40,569 --> 00:03:44,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">each at three correlation levels, zero, point four,
and point eight,</font>

59
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,071
<font color="#FFFFFF">averaged over twenty repetitions.</font>

60
00:03:47,151 --> 00:03:53,522
<font color="#FFFFFF">They then test twelve real data sets for both
ℓ1-regularized least-squares and logistic regression,</font>

61
00:03:53,602 --> 00:04:00,625
<font color="#FFFFFF">ranging from small gene-expression matrices up to
problems with millions of features such as news20 and rcv1.</font>

62
00:04:00,705 --> 00:04:04,340
<font color="#FFFFFF">Baselines are the working-set strategy, Celer, and Blitz.</font>

63
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:06,306
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results are decisive.</font>

64
00:04:06,386 --> 00:04:11,369
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across every simulated configuration, the Hessian
rule takes the least time,</font>

65
00:04:11,449 --> 00:04:15,632
<font color="#FFFFFF">and its advantage is largest exactly where
competitors struggle,</font>

66
00:04:15,712 --> 00:04:18,697
<font color="#FFFFFF">the high-correlation, low-dimensional setting.</font>

67
00:04:18,777 --> 00:04:21,828
<font color="#FFFFFF">On real data it wins on nearly all twelve sets.</font>

68
00:04:21,908 --> 00:04:25,759
<font color="#FFFFFF">For ℓ1-regularized least-squares it is fastest on
all five,</font>

69
00:04:25,839 --> 00:04:31,288
<font color="#FFFFFF">and in all but one case it finishes in under half
the time of the next-best method,</font>

70
00:04:31,368 --> 00:04:32,954
<font color="#FFFFFF">the working-set strategy.</font>

71
00:04:33,034 --> 00:04:40,682
<font color="#FFFFFF">On YearPredictionMSD it fits the full path in
seventy-nine seconds against five hundred forty-one for the runner-up,</font>

72
00:04:40,762 --> 00:04:45,412
<font color="#FFFFFF">and on e2006-tfidf in fourteen seconds against one
hundred forty-three,</font>

73
00:04:45,492 --> 00:04:48,010
<font color="#FFFFFF">speedups of roughly seven to ten times.</font>

74
00:04:48,390 --> 00:04:51,685
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two component studies show where the gains come from.</font>

75
00:04:51,765 --> 00:04:54,168
<font color="#FFFFFF">Looking at the warm start in isolation,</font>

76
00:04:54,248 --> 00:05:01,236
<font color="#FFFFFF">on colon-cancer and YearPredictionMSD the Hessian
warm start collapses the number of coordinate-descent passes,</font>

77
00:05:01,316 --> 00:05:03,592
<font color="#FFFFFF">frequently to a single pass per step,</font>

78
00:05:03,672 --> 00:05:09,513
<font color="#FFFFFF">because when the active set does not change the
warm start is essentially the exact solution.</font>

79
00:05:09,593 --> 00:05:11,678
<font color="#FFFFFF">Looking at screening in isolation,</font>

80
00:05:11,758 --> 00:05:17,536
<font color="#FFFFFF">the Hessian rule keeps the number of retained
predictors close to the true active-set floor,</font>

81
00:05:17,616 --> 00:05:21,994
<font color="#FFFFFF">while alternatives like Celer, Blitz, the strong
rule, EDPP, Gap Safe,</font>

82
00:05:22,074 --> 00:05:25,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">and Sasvi retain orders of magnitude more predictors,</font>

83
00:05:25,448 --> 00:05:28,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the gap widens as correlation increases.</font>

84
00:05:28,550 --> 00:05:33,181
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is that a single idea, reusing
second-order Hessian information,</font>

85
00:05:33,261 --> 00:05:34,404
<font color="#FFFFFF">pays off twice over.</font>

86
00:05:34,484 --> 00:05:38,197
<font color="#FFFFFF">It tightens screening so the solver sees far fewer
predictors,</font>

87
00:05:38,277 --> 00:05:43,214
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it supplies warm starts so accurate that many
path steps converge in one pass.</font>

88
00:05:43,294 --> 00:05:48,536
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together these make the Hessian Screening Rule the
fastest method for fitting lasso and</font>

89
00:05:48,616 --> 00:05:52,818
<font color="#FFFFFF">ℓ1-regularized logistic regression paths across the
benchmarks tested,</font>

90
00:05:52,898 --> 00:05:59,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the biggest edge in the high-correlation
regime that has historically been the hardest for screening rules.</font>
